  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in New Mexico total $13.2B for FY2026 to date, across 7,317 awards with an average award size of $1.8M. Obligations are down 1.8% year over year. The Department of Energy dominates buying with $9.8B in obligations, and Facilities Support Services is the largest industry by dollar value at $9.7B. Two contractors account for a large share of spending: TRIAD NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC at $5.1B and NATIONAL TECHNOLOGY & ENGINEERING SOLUTIONS OF SANDIA, LLC at $4.5B. Year-by-year totals show a steady rise from $11.6B in FY21 to $13.5B in FY25, with the current fiscal year at $13.2B. Outside the headline DOE work, the Department of Homeland Security accounts for $1.9B and the Department of Defense accounts for $805M. Top non-energy industries include Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $1.9B and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $239M. These figures are all contract obligations and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in New Mexico trended recently?",
        "body": "Obligations rose from $11.6B in FY21 to $13.5B in FY25, with FY26 at $13.2B. Obligations are down 1.8% year over year. The recent year-to-year movement reflects a small pullback from the FY25 peak. Fiscal-year totals presented here are partial for FY2026 to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What sectors and vendors are driving spending in New Mexico?",
        "body": "Facilities Support Services leads industries with $9.7B in obligations, followed by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $1.9B. TRIAD NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC and NATIONAL TECHNOLOGY & ENGINEERING SOLUTIONS OF SANDIA, LLC lead vendors with $5.1B and $4.5B respectively, and FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO follows with $1.8B. The Department of Energy is the top buyer at $9.8B, concentrating much of that spending."
      }
    ]
  },
